# Harness integration

`kbw integrate --generate` renders one canonical skill from `core/skills/` and
`project/skill-config/skill.toml`; `{{kb_path}}/kbw integrate --apply` installs it into the
host repository. The same `SKILL.md` (front matter `name: kb` plus `description`) is valid
for all three harnesses.

| harness | skill location in the host | instruction file | documentation |
|---|---|---|---|
| Claude Code | `.claude/skills/kb/SKILL.md` | `CLAUDE.md` | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills |
| Codex | `.agents/skills/kb/SKILL.md` | `AGENTS.md` | https://developers.openai.com/codex/skills (now served at https://developers.openai.com/codex/build-skills) |
| Cursor | `.agents/skills/kb/SKILL.md`, or `.claude/skills/kb/SKILL.md` when Claude Code is enabled | `AGENTS.md` | https://cursor.com/docs/skills |

Discovery facts from that documentation (checked 2026-09-27; re-check when a harness
changes):

- **Claude Code** loads project skills from `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` in the start
  directory and every parent up to the repository root. All front matter fields are
  optional (`description` is recommended; `name` defaults to the directory name; unknown
  fields are ignored). Supporting files referenced from `SKILL.md` are read only when
  needed. Edits to `SKILL.md` are picked up during a session.
- **Codex** scans `.agents/skills` in every directory from the current working directory up
  to the repository root. `SKILL.md` must include `name` and `description`; an optional
  `agents/openai.yaml` adds UI metadata and invocation policy (kb does not generate one).
  Codex reads `AGENTS.md` instructions from the repository root down to the working
  directory.
- **Cursor** reads `.agents/skills` and `.cursor/skills`, and for compatibility also
  `.claude/skills` and `.codex/skills`. `name` is required, must use lowercase letters,
  digits and hyphens, and must match the folder name; `description` is required. Cursor
  reads `AGENTS.md` in the project root and in subdirectories.

## Avoiding duplicate skills

- claude + cursor: only `.claude/skills/kb/` is installed; Cursor reads it for
  compatibility, so no `.agents/skills/kb/` copy is made.
- cursor alone, or codex (with or without cursor): `.agents/skills/kb/` is installed.
- claude + codex (+ cursor): both directories are installed because Claude Code and Codex
  each read only their own. Cursor then discovers two identical `kb` skills; how it
  deduplicates them is not documented. If that matters for your team, enable only the
  harnesses you use in `project/skill-config/skill.toml` (for example drop `claude` for a
  Codex + Cursor team, or drop `codex` for a Claude Code + Cursor team).

## Managed blocks and the lock

`AGENTS.md` and `CLAUDE.md` are changed only between the lines
`<!-- kb:begin kb-instructions -->` and `<!-- kb:end kb-instructions -->`; every other byte
is preserved, and the file is created if missing. `.kbw/integration.lock` in the host
records the hashes kb installed. A generated file or block edited by hand is reported as
`CONFLICT` and is never overwritten silently (`--apply --force` overwrites it explicitly).
`{{kb_path}}/kbw integrate --check` fails with `DRIFT_DETECTED` when the host differs from
the committed bundle.

## Limits

Instruction files and skills are text: they make the protocol available to an agent, but
they do not guarantee that it is followed. kb relies on no undocumented harness hooks.
Updating a skill file does not update instructions already loaded by a running agent; a
`SKILL_OUTDATED` error tells the agent to re-read the skill or start a new session.
